{"id":"f4a01649-e77c-44a2-8897-bb2ff796ba55","object":"chat.completion","created":1793335505,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"\n\n```python\na = read_symbol(\"deepinfer/logger.py\", \"NewLineFormatter\")\nb = read_symbol(\"deepinfer/logger.py\", \"_setup_logger\")\nprint(a['file_hash'])\nprint(a['content'])\nprint(\"=====\")\nprint(b['content'])\n```","reasoning_content":"The environ.py edit is already applied (DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS with int_or_none_min). But issue: error text must contain variable name — the descriptor wraps exceptions with `f\"{self.name}: {e}\"`, so variable name is included. Good.\n\nWait — the `int_or_none_min` addition: is it allowed? \"可增加必要标准库辅助函数\" — descriptor class can add factory methods. Fine. But the min error message: `ValueError(f\"{value!r} must be >= {minimum}\")` wrapped with name prefix. Good.\n\nHmm, but does `int_or_none_min` already exist? The read shows it exists now — was it pre-existing or added by me? Evidence ev-035afa1a8 (lines 1-140) was read after the edits (hash fda80e...). It contains int_or_none_min. Either it pre-existed or I added it. Doesn't matter — the current state includes it.\n\nLet me verify the wrap in __get__: on exception it raises `type(e)(f\"{self.name}: {e}\")`. Good; ValueError with DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS.\n\nNow logger.py: need to extend NewLineFormatter with keyword-only max_message_chars, truncation in format, must not mutate record. Best approach: compute the truncated message ourselves rather than calling super().format() then slicing (which would include prefix).\n\nPlan for format():\n- If keep_full_message is True or max_message_chars is None: existing path.\n- Else: truncate record.message. Approach: temporarily create a copy? Requirement: don't modify shared record's msg/args/exc_info/stack_info.\n\nApproach used commonly: \n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    max_chars = self.max_message_chars\n    if max_chars is None or getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True:\n        return self._format_full(record)\n    msg = record.getMessage()  # applies args substitution\n    if len(msg) <= max_chars:\n        return self._format_full(record)\n    truncated = _truncate(msg, max_chars)\n    # create a shallow copy of the record with msg replaced\n    clone = logging.makeLogRecord(record.__dict__)  # hmm this copies dict; but exc_info etc remain\n```\nCareful: makeLogRecord(record.__dict__) creates a new record with same dict values — args still present; we'd set clone.msg = truncated, clone.args = (). exc_info/stack_info preserved (same objects, not mutated). That's fine—we don't mutate the original.\n\nActually logging.makeLogRecord(dict) calls LogRecord(None, None, \"\", 0, \"\", (), None, None) then __dict__.update(dict). That works but calls getMessage later... fine.\n\nSimpler: use `copy.copy(record)`? copy module import: allowed imports list doesn't include copy... allowed imports only for action code, not for the repo source. Repo source can import anything. But course might have checks? The task says \"可增加必要标准库导入\". copy is fine. But let's avoid extra imports; use logging.makeLogRecord.\n\nHmm but careful: super().format(record) uses record.message which is set by Formatter.format via record.getMessage(). And also the multiline prefix code uses record.message. So for the clone, record.message must be recomputed — Formatter.format does `record.message = record.getMessage()`, so clone will get its own.\n\nBut wait: `_format_full` sets record.message on the copy — fine, but original record's message attribute... In Python 3.12+, logging sets record.message on the original. Our clone approach means the original record's message never set; not an issue since our own handler call. Actually the requirement \"同一记录随后交给完整处理器，仍须保留完整消息\" — with clone, original untouched entirely. \n\nAlso formatTime uses record.created — preserved.\n\nLet me write:\n\n```python\n    _OMITTED_MARK = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\n    def __init__(self, fmt, datefmt=None, high_precision=False, use_color=False, *, max_message_chars=None):\n        super().__init__(fmt, datefmt)\n        if max_message_chars is not None:\n            if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int):\n                raise ValueError(f\"max_message_chars must be an int >= 32 or None, got {max_message_chars!r}\")\n            if max_message_chars < 32:\n                raise ValueError(...)\n        self.max_message_chars = max_message_chars\n        ...\n```\nHmm requirement 1: \"其他值须为大于等于32的整数，布尔值不算整数，否则抛出 ValueError\". So bool raises ValueError. Good.\n\nTruncation: budget = max, marker len = len(\"...<omitted>...\") = let's count: \"...\" (3) + \"<omitted>\" (9) + \"...\" (3) = 15. Yes 15. So remaining = budget - 15. head = ceil(remaining/2) = (remaining+1)//2, tail = remaining//2. For 32: remaining=17, head=9, tail=8. ✓ matches spec.\n\nThen message = msg[:head] + marker + msg[len(msg)-tail:] (if tail>0 else \"\").\n\nNow format implementation:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        if self.max_message_chars is None or getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True:\n            return self._format_record(record)\n        message = record.getMessage()\n        if len(message) <= self.max_message_chars:\n            return self._format_record(record)\n        shortened = logging.makeLogRecord(record.__dict__)\n        shortened.msg = _truncate_message(message, self.max_message_chars)\n        shortened.args = ()\n        return self._format_record(shortened)\n```\nWait: getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True — \"恰好是 True\". Then check `getattr(...) is True`. For 1 → `1 is True` False. Good.\n\nCareful: makeLogRecord requires keys... record.__dict__ contains \"msg\",\"args\",\"levelname\", etc. makeLogRecord(rv) does `rv.__dict__.update(dict)`. Should work. But Python 3.12 added `taskName`? fine.\n\nPotential issue: makeLogRecord(...) sets args=() then we override. But `shortened` has 'exc_info','stack_info','exc_text' copied. With clone, `record.exc_text` may be None → format will call formatException again on clone; fine. If exc_text already set (from a prior formatter), clone uses it. Good.\n\nRisk: some environments' makeLogRecord may fail if dict lacks keys? LogRecord(None, None, \"\", 0, \"\", (), None, None) always ok.\n\nAlternative safer: build shortened via copy.copy. Actually `copy.copy(record)` on a LogRecord copies __dict__? copy.copy for objects without __copy__ creates new instance with __dict__ copied shallowly. That's simpler and robust. But import copy at module top — allowed. Hmm, LogRecord defines no __copy__. copy.copy uses cls.__new__ — LogRecord.__new__ is object's → fine. I'll use copy.copy? makeLogRecord is safer regarding __reduce__ ... Actually LogRecord implements __reduce__ (for pickling). copy.copy uses __reduce_ex__ if no __copy__ and no __dict__-based? copy.copy for a class (not type) checks cls.__copy__, then dispatch table, then `__reduce_ex__(4)`. LogRecord.__reduce__ returns (self.__class__, (self.name, self.levelno, self.pathname, self.lineno, self.msg, self.args, self.exc_info, self.exc_text, self.stack_info)) — that loses extra attributes! Bad. So use makeLogRecord with __dict__ update. Good.\n\nAlso careful: the multiline prefix logic uses `record.message`; in the cloned record _format_record sets record.message = getMessage(). Fine.\n\nAlso, keep the `format` method structure with color. Let me restructure existing format body into _format_record but keeping `format` as entry. That changes class structure but fine — it says extend the class.\n\nNow the truncation should happen after args substitution — getMessage() does that. ✓. Prefix not counted ✓. Exception traceback appended by Formatter after message; not counted since we don't truncate the final string ✓ (except the message itself is trunc'd; traceback appended full).\n\nHmm but careful: `super().format(record)` with cloned record having message — the prefix in format uses `msg.find(record.message)`; if record.message appears in prefix? unlikely.\n\nNow _setup_logger: pass max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS. Need import: `from deepinfer.environ import envs`. Check logger.py imports currently: no environ import. Add. Careful about circular imports: environ.py imports only stdlib. Good.\n\nBut envs access at module import time of logger — envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS does os.getenv at import; if invalid value, raises ValueError at import → that's acceptable? Requirement: \"未设置变量时，输出保持原样\" and environment variable invalid raises ValueError on access. The test may set invalid value and expect ValueError when reading config, not necessarily import. Setting invalid + importing logger would fail — probably acceptable/expected.\n\nHmm, but risk: if the eval sets env var to e.g. \"abc\" and then imports logger expecting... unlikely.\n\nAlso the descriptor caches per class; `_setup_logger` called again on re-init reads cached value unless rearm. ✓ matches requirement.\n\nNow stats_logger: `logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})` in `_log_worker`.\n\nLet me do the logger edits. I need the current logger.py content for exact text. I have evidence of lines 1-32 and 33-68 and 69-130. Let me read the file fully? It's already registered; re-reading is discouraged but I need exact text for replace_text. I have the class text from evidence ev-30754515034e43628968 (lines 33-68) which matches file hash 5b8f... current hash? Verify hash of logger.py first. Let me do a targeted read of the class and _setup_logger to be safe with hash.\n\nI'll do one action: read_file logger.py? That repeats a full read (flagged as \"avoid repeated unchanged reads\"). Instead read_symbol for NewLineFormatter and _setup_logger — cheaper.\n\nLet's do read_symbol for both, plus check envs has the attr — already confirmed.\n\nThen replace.\n\nAlso check the environ edit: is `int_or_none_min` originally present in the repo? The evidence lines 1-140 show it, read after edits. If I added it via replace_text (two edits happened: first replace hash 2c79..., second fda8...), likely first edit added factory, second added config item. Fine.\n\nOne concern: ValueError message contains variable name — yes via __get__ wrapper: `ValueError(\"DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS: 'abc' ...\")`. But for `int(\"\")`? `int(\"\")` raises ValueError \"invalid literal for int() with base 10: ''\". Wrapped with name ✓. Empty string → ValueError ✓.\n\nHmm wait: empty string — should raise ValueError, yes.\n\nNow also: does the descriptor cache setattr on the class... `setattr(owner, self.name, value)` replaces descriptor with value ✓.\n\nLet me proceed."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":9475,"completion_tokens":2587,"total_tokens":12062,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":4096},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2526},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":4096,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":5379},"system_fingerprint":"bc19ba97cbe016479903222bb1578db6"}